{% comment %}
  sections/footer.liquid

  The footer, composed rather than configured.

  ## The hierarchy

      Column  ›  Group  ›  Elements

  The section holds Columns and Utility rows; a Column holds Groups and elements;
  a Group bundles what belongs together. Every level is a real theme block, so a
  merchant rearranges the whole footer by dragging, and nothing here needs to
  know what a "newsletter column" is.

  What the section owns is now only what is true of the footer as a whole: its
  surface, its corners, its border, the panel the columns sit in, and the policy
  row. The contact details, the pickers, the payment marks and the copyright are
  blocks — they were section settings, which meant the section decided both what
  they said and where they went.

  ## Why this list has no `@theme` in it

  `@theme` means "every public theme block", and a `blocks` array that contains
  it stops honouring the individual types listed beside it — which is every
  private block this footer is built from. That is why the theme editor offered
  no Column, no Brand and address, no Utilities row: they were listed, and the
  `@theme` sitting in the same array meant only public blocks were ever shown.

  `blocks/mega-menu.liquid` is the pattern that has always worked here: it names
  every child type it accepts and never reaches for `@theme`.

  So the public blocks that belong in a footer are named one by one instead. It
  is a longer list and a shorter answer: a merchant sees exactly what is useful
  here, private pieces included.

  ## Two layouts, and what the card actually wraps

  Every block a merchant adds arrives through one `content_for 'blocks'`, so
  they are all siblings in one container and nothing here can wrap a subset of
  them in a `<div>`. That matters, because the card belongs around the columns
  and the policy links — not around the contact bar or the bottom bar, which the
  design draws as their own surfaces below it.

  Grid solves it without extra markup. The panel is the grid, every block is a
  grid item, and the card is a `::before` pseudo-element — itself a grid item —
  spanning from the first row down to the row before the first utility row. So
  the card is a real painted box around exactly the right region, and anything
  after it sits outside on the footer's own ground.

      Card    a surface with a border and rounded corners, and the divisions
              inside it drawn: a vertical rule between the menus and the aside,
              and a line above the policy links.

      Plain   no surface and no rules. Nothing moves — the columns keep their
              widths, the policy links keep their place — the panel is simply
              left open.

  That used to be two settings: a panel *style* that drew the surface and a
  panel *layout* that drew the rules. They were never usefully independent — a
  bordered card with no divisions in it, or divisions floating with no card
  around them, is not a thing anyone asked for — so it is one control now.

  ## The background comes from the colour scheme

  Both the colour and the gradient. `.color-scheme-container` paints
  `--color-background` and `--gradient-background`, and the gradient half is a
  real setting on every scheme — so a merchant who wants the footer's deep green
  ramp sets it once in Theme settings › Colours and the footer follows. There is
  no gradient defined in this theme's stylesheet for it to disagree with.

  ## Collapsing

  One `<footer-columns>` — an accordion that also measures its own grid —
  wraps the blocks, always. It used to be behind a
  "Collapse columns on mobile" checkbox with a breakpoint slider beside it — two
  settings for a decision no merchant makes differently, and a footer that reads
  as a wall of links on a phone when either one is wrong. The breakpoint is the
  theme's own.

  ## Motion

  `<motion-effect>` is the theme's reveal engine and nothing here re-implements
  it. The panel and the utility rows each get one with rising delays, so the
  footer arrives as one short cascade rather than several unrelated animations.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign st = section.settings

  comment
    The background is the colour scheme's until the merchant says otherwise:
    either a colour of their own, or a picture behind the whole footer.

    `snippets/background-media.liquid` owns the picture — the absolute layer,
    the mobile crop, cover-vs-fit and the stacking order — so this section adds
    the settings and the `has-background-media` class and nothing else. The
    colour is emitted as a real `background` declaration rather than a custom
    property because `color-scheme-container` paints a background *and* a
    gradient image, and only the shorthand clears both.
  endcomment
  assign background_media = st.background_media | default: 'none'
  assign has_background_media = false
  if background_media != 'none'
    assign has_background_media = true
  endif

  assign background_paint = ''
  if has_background_media == false
    assign background_paint = st.background_color | default: ''
  endif

  assign border_width = 0
  if st.border_style == 'solid'
    assign border_width = st.border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0

    comment
      Opacity goes into the colour, not onto the element: `opacity` on the
      footer would fade every column along with the frame.

      With no colour picked the border is `currentColor`, which the colour
      filters cannot take an alpha channel from — there is no channel to read
      until the scheme resolves it in the browser. `color-mix` does the same
      fade at that point instead, so both paths honour the slider.
    endcomment
    assign border_percent = st.border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if st.border_color == blank
      assign border_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: border_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign border_alpha = border_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign border_paint = st.border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', border_alpha
    endif
  endif

  comment
    The panel's geometry is measured in the browser, not worked out here.

    Every block a merchant adds arrives through one `content_for 'blocks'`, and
    a section cannot iterate those blocks — `section.blocks` returns nothing for
    them, exactly as sections/header.liquid already records. So the track list,
    the rows the card covers and how far the policy links run cannot be derived
    in Liquid however this file is written. An earlier version of this section
    tried, found no columns, and fell back to a single track: every column in
    the footer stacked on top of the next.

    The footer-columns element in assets/modules.js reads the same three
    numbers off the rendered children, where they can actually be seen. Each column publishes
    its own width share, so nothing here has to know how many there are.

    What this file still decides is what it genuinely owns: whether there is a
    policy row at all, which is a section setting rather than a block.
  endcomment
  assign columns_id = 'FooterColumns-' | append: section.id

  assign has_policy = false
  if st.policy_menu != blank and st.policy_menu.links.size > 0
    assign has_policy = true
  endif

  comment
    Spacing is asked for as declarations, not as its own attribute. This element
    already has a style attribute of its own, and a tag with two of them keeps
    the first and drops the second — which is how a padding slider ends up
    moving a value that never reaches the page. The data-section-spacing hook is
    written by hand below because that attribute, not the properties, is what
    base.css selects on.
  endcomment
  capture section_spacing
    render 'spacing', section: st, declarations: true
  endcapture
-%}

{%- capture footer_styles -%}
  --footer-radius:
    {{ st.radius_top_left | default: 0 }}px
    {{ st.radius_top_right | default: 0 }}px
    {{ st.radius_bottom_right | default: 0 }}px
    {{ st.radius_bottom_left | default: 0 }}px;
  --footer-margin-block-start: {{ st.margin_top | default: 0 }}px;
  --footer-margin-block-end: {{ st.margin_bottom | default: 0 }}px;
  {%- if border_width > 0 -%}
    --footer-border-width: {{ border_width }}px;
    --footer-border-color: {{ border_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if background_paint != '' -%}
    background: {{ background_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {{- section_spacing | strip -}}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `color-scheme-container` is what paints the scheme's background *and* its
  gradient. The class was missing, so the footer took only the text colours and
  its background came from a gradient written into the stylesheet — which no
  merchant could reach.
{%- endcomment -%}
<footer
  class="footer color-scheme-container color-{{ st.color_scheme }}{% if has_background_media %} has-background-media{% endif %}"
  role="contentinfo"
  data-section-spacing
  style="{{ footer_styles | strip_newlines | strip }}"
>
  {%- if has_background_media -%}
    {% render 'background-media', settings: st %}
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="{% render 'container', width: st.container_width %}">
    {%- if section.blocks.size > 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The accordion and the grid are the same element now.

        They used to be nested — a panel wrapping an accordion wrapping the
        blocks — which put the contact bar and the bottom bar inside the card
        along with the columns. One container, with the card painted by a
        pseudo-element over part of it, is what lets the same set of siblings be
        partly inside the card and partly below it.

        The accordion behaviour is unaffected: it only ever looked for the
        `<details>` elements it owns, and those are the columns.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <motion-effect
        class="footer__panel footer__panel--{{ st.panel_style | default: 'card' }}"
        data-easing="outQuint"
        {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', section: st, children: true %}
      >
        <footer-columns
          id="{{ columns_id }}"
          class="footer__columns"
          {% if has_policy %}data-has-policy{% endif %}
          data-collapse-below="750"
        >
          {% content_for 'blocks' %}

          {%- comment -%}
            The policy row lives *inside* the columns, not under the bottom bar.

            It was in the copyright row, which is where a theme puts it when the
            footer is a stack of bars. This footer is a panel of columns, and the
            design puts the policy links on their own line directly beneath the
            menu columns, above the rule. Rendering it here — as the last item in
            the same flex container — is what makes it share the columns' grid
            and sit under the menus rather than under the whole panel.

            It stays a section setting rather than a block: a merchant can
            rearrange every other part of this footer by dragging, and the one
            row a store selling anything is obliged to show is not something to
            be able to drag out by accident. `--column-order: 2` puts it after
            the columns (which run -3 … 1) and before the utility rows.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if has_policy -%}
            <div class="footer__policies" style="--column-order: 2;">
              <ul class="footer__policies-list" role="list">
                {%- for link in st.policy_menu.links -%}
                  <li>
                    <a class="footer__policy-link" href="{{ link.url }}">{{ link.title }}</a>
                  </li>
                {%- endfor -%}
              </ul>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </footer-columns>

        {%- comment -%}
          The desktop state of the columns, decided before the first paint.

          Above the breakpoint a footer column is not a disclosure at all — it is
          a headed list, always open. `<accordion-element>` is what opens them,
          and it cannot run until its module has loaded, so the browser paints
          four collapsed rows first and the page jumps when the script catches
          up. That is the flash on reload.

          It cannot be fixed with CSS alone: a closed `<details>` hides its
          content in the user agent stylesheet, and no rule reliably reopens it
          across browsers — the `open` attribute has to actually change. So it
          changes here, in a parser-blocking script sitting immediately after the
          markup it acts on, which means it lands in the same paint.

          Below the breakpoint nothing is touched: the columns are authored
          closed, which is already correct, and the component takes over from
          there. With scripting off, `<details>` still opens on tap.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <script>
          (function () {
            var el = document.getElementById({{ columns_id | json }});
            if (!el || !window.matchMedia('(min-width: 750px)').matches) return;

            var items = el.querySelectorAll('details.footer-column');
            for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
              items[i].open = true;
              var summary = items[i].querySelector('summary');
              if (!summary) continue;
              // Not a control at this width, so not a tab stop either. The
              // component sets the same two attributes when it upgrades.
              summary.setAttribute('data-static', '');
              summary.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');
            }

            el.setAttribute('data-static', '');
          })();
        </script>
      </motion-effect>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</footer>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.footer.name",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "_footer-column"
    },
    {
      "type": "_footer-contact"
    },
    {
      "type": "_footer-utilities"
    },
    {
      "type": "_footer-legal"
    },
    {
      "type": "group"
    },
    {
      "type": "menu"
    },
    {
      "type": "email-signup"
    },
    {
      "type": "social-follow"
    },
    {
      "type": "social-icons"
    },
    {
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "type": "image"
    },
    {
      "type": "button"
    },
    {
      "type": "buttons"
    },
    {
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "type": "spacer"
    },
    {
      "type": "icon-with-text"
    },
    {
      "type": "custom-html"
    },
    {
      "type": "custom-liquid"
    },
    {
      "type": "@app"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "container_width",
      "label": "t:shared.container.width.label",
      "info": "t:shared.container.width.info",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "edge",
          "label": "t:shared.container.width.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "wide",
          "label": "t:shared.container.width.options__2.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "standard",
          "label": "t:shared.container.width.options__3.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "compact",
          "label": "t:shared.container.width.options__4.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "custom",
          "label": "t:shared.container.width.options__5.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "standard"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "panel_style",
      "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.panel_style.label",
      "info": "t:sections.footer.settings.panel_style.info",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "card",
          "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.panel_style.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "plain",
          "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.panel_style.options__2.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "card"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:sections.footer.headers.legal_links"
    },
    {
      "type": "link_list",
      "id": "policy_menu",
      "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.policy_menu.label"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:shared.appearance.header"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "t:shared.color_scheme.label",
      "default": "scheme-2"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "background_media",
      "label": "t:shared.background.media.label",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "none",
          "label": "t:shared.background.media.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "image",
          "label": "t:shared.background.media.options__2.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "none"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_background",
      "id": "background_color",
      "label": "t:shared.background.color.label",
      "info": "t:shared.background.color.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.background_media == 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image",
      "label": "t:shared.background.image.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.background_media == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image_mobile",
      "label": "t:shared.background.image_mobile.label",
      "info": "t:shared.background.image_mobile.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.background_media == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "image_position",
      "label": "t:shared.background.image_position.label",
      "info": "t:shared.background.image_position.info",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "cover",
          "label": "t:shared.background.image_position.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "fit",
          "label": "t:shared.background.image_position.options__2.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "cover",
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.background_media == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:blocks.group.headers.borders"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "border_style",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.border_style.label",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "none",
          "label": "t:shared.options.none"
        },
        {
          "value": "solid",
          "label": "t:shared.options.solid"
        }
      ],
      "default": "none"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "border_width",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.border_width.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 12,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 1,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.border_style == 'solid' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "border_opacity",
      "label": "t:shared.border.opacity.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 5,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 100,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.border_style == 'solid' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "border_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.border_color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.border_style == 'solid' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:sections.footer.headers.corners"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "radius_top_left",
      "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.radius_top_left.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 40
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "radius_top_right",
      "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.radius_top_right.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 40
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "radius_bottom_left",
      "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.radius_bottom_left.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "radius_bottom_right",
      "label": "t:sections.footer.settings.radius_bottom_right.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:shared.animation.header"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "animation_text",
      "label": "t:shared.animation.animation_text.label",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "inherit",
          "label": "t:shared.animation.animation_text.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "none",
          "label": "t:shared.animation.animation_text.options__2.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "fade-in",
          "label": "t:shared.animation.animation_text.options__3.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "fade-up",
          "label": "t:shared.animation.animation_text.options__4.label"
        },
